# 🛍️ Olist Customer Satisfaction Dashboard

**PORA Academy · Cohort 7 · Data Analysis · Group 1 Capstone**

A decision-first Streamlit dashboard built on **99,441 Olist orders (2016–2018)**
that identifies what *actually* drives customer satisfaction — and quantifies
how much it's worth.

> **Primary finding.** Delivery punctuality is the **#1 satisfaction lever**.
> Late deliveries cost an average of **1.73★** — a **40.2 % drop** versus
> on-time orders. This gap dwarfs every geographic, product, or seasonal
> difference in the dataset.

🔗 **Live app:** https://pora-customer-satisfaction-dashboard-wu3ymdqefkytau38tt7mss.streamlit.app/

---

## 📖 Why this dashboard exists

E-commerce teams tend to optimise for *raw speed*. This analysis shows the
thing that actually drives loyalty is **reliability** — hitting the promised
delivery date. The dashboard walks a stakeholder from headline KPIs, down
through statistically verified root causes, to a **prioritised, ROI-ranked
action list**. Every number on screen reconciles to a verified SQL reference
(see [`docs/reconciliation.md`](#) or the *Detail & Method* tab).

---

## 🎯 What you can do in the app

| Tab | What it answers | What you take away |
|---|---|---|
| **📈 Overview** | How big is the business, how happy are customers? | Baseline KPIs + review-score distribution |
| **⭐ Customer Satisfaction** | *Where* is dissatisfaction concentrated? | Delivery-time buckets, state comparisons, monthly trend, Brazil map |
| **🔬 Detail & Method** | *How strong* is the evidence? | Welch's t-test, Cohen's *d*, 95 % CI, sensitivity table, and 5 named limitations |
| **💡 Recommendations** | *What should we do first?* | Impact × Effort priority matrix + 5 actionable recommendations with owners & KPIs |

Key interactive features:

- **Global filters** — Year and State selectors that update **every KPI and chart** on the dashboard.
- **Dose–response analysis** — Satisfaction collapses as delays grow (On-Time → Slightly Late → Moderately Late → Severely Late).
- **Statistical rigor** — Welch's *t*-test, Cohen's *d*, and 95 % CIs on every delivery-gap claim.
- **What-if simulator** — Project the average score if X % of late orders convert to on-time.
- **Geographic insights** — Custom-rendered Brazil map highlighting review concentration and lagging states (e.g., Rio de Janeiro, Bahia).

---

## 🚀 Quick start

### Run locally

```bash
# 1. Clone
git clone https://github.com/<your-org>/<your-repo>.git
cd <your-repo>

# 2. Install
python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate   # Windows: .venv\Scripts\activate
pip install -r requirements.txt

# 3. Get the data
#    Download the four Olist CSVs from Kaggle:
#    https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/olistbr/brazilian-ecommerce
#    and place them in ./data/ (see tree below).

# 4. Run
streamlit run olist_app.py
```

The app opens at <http://localhost:8501>.

### Deploy to Streamlit Community Cloud

1. Push the repo (with `data/` inside) to GitHub.
2. In Streamlit Cloud → **New app** → pick the repo, branch, and `olist_app.py`.
3. *If the CSVs are not in the repo* (they are ~130 MB), mount them via
   **Settings → Secrets**:

   ```toml
   OLIST_DATA_DIR = "/mount/data/olist"
   ```

   and add one line near the top of `olist_app.py`:

   ```python
   import streamlit as st
   if "OLIST_DATA_DIR" in st.secrets:
       os.environ["OLIST_DATA_DIR"] = st.secrets["OLIST_DATA_DIR"]
   ```

4. Deploy. Cold start is ~30 s; warm start ~1 s.

### `requirements.txt`

```text
streamlit
pandas
matplotlib
requests
plotly
numpy
scipy
```

---

## 📊 What responds to the filters

The sidebar **Reporting Year** and **State** selectors apply to **every KPI
and chart** on the dashboard. Set both filters to **All** to reproduce the
reference numbers in the sidebar.

| Element | Filtered? |
|---|---|
| Hero strip + headline finding banner | ✅ Yes |
| KPI row (orders, delivered, avg review, late, avg delivery days) | ✅ Yes |
| Review score distribution | ✅ Yes |
| Monthly average review score | ✅ Yes |
| Average review score by state (top 10) | ✅ Yes |
| On-time vs late by state (top 5) | ✅ Yes |
| Delivery punctuality vs satisfaction (+ what-if) | ✅ Yes |
| Satisfaction by delivery-time bucket (reference line = selection average) | ✅ Yes |
| Brazil map (selected states coloured, others greyed; year filtered) | ✅ Yes |
| Insight line under every chart | ✅ Yes — recomputed from the selection |
| Detail & Method diagnostic tables | ❌ No — whole-dataset data-quality checks |
| Recommendations tab | ❌ No — decisions use the whole dataset |

All filtered queries share one function (`apply_filters()` in `olist_app.py`),
so every chart filters identically — no drift between tabs.

---

## 🎨 Design system

The dashboard uses a purpose-built design system tuned for decision-making:

| Token | Value | Role |
|---|---|---|
| Brand | `#0EA5E9` (Sky 500) | Interactive — tabs, sliders, focus rings |
| Accent | `#0F766E` (Teal 700) | Brand accent on hero gradient |
| Success | `#059669` (Emerald 600) | "On-time", positive delta |
| Warning | `#D97706` (Amber 600) | "Slightly late", caution |
| Danger | `#DC2626` (Red 600) | "Severely late", negative delta |
| Neutrals | Tailwind **Slate** (50 → 900) | Backgrounds, text, dividers |

**Typography** — Inter with a fluid `clamp()` scale (body `0.94–1.0 rem`,
KPI values `1.4–1.85 rem`, hero `2.0–2.75 rem`), so the dashboard reads
cleanly on laptops, projectors, and 4K displays alike.

**Spacing** — 8-pt grid; card padding `24 px`, card gap `16 px`,
section gap `32 px`.

**Accessibility** — all body text meets WCAG AA on white; `:focus-visible`
outlines on every interactive element; `prefers-reduced-motion` respected.

---

## ⚠️ Limitations (read before quoting)

The *Detail & Method* tab contains the full list (L1–L5). The two that matter
most for interpreting the headline finding:

- **L1 — Association, not causation.** The 1.73★ on-time↔late gap is a
  *strong association*, not proof that lateness alone causes lower ratings.
  Product quality, seller performance, distance, and communication failures
  all contribute. A controlled pilot is needed before claiming a precise lift.
- **L2 — Incomplete coverage & data grain.** 2016 has only Sept/Oct/Dec
  (no November); 2018 ends **17 October** — **2017 is the only complete
  calendar year**. And the review table has **99,224 rows across 98,673
  orders** (551 fan-out rows); every average here is row-level. Per-order
  dedupe shifts means by < 0.01★, so the headline gap holds, but any figure
  quoted alongside a row count must state which grain it uses.

The remaining limitations (L3–L5) cover *delivered ≠ delivered-with-a-date*
semantics, dataset scope & external validity, and small-selection noise.

